  "statusText": "Mapped policy state: EA_ON. Tail risk sits in the low band while stress-side conditions stay high on an unresolved Middle East supply disruption and a weekend price-discovery gap.",
  "recommendation": "Tail remains well below the block threshold, so the deterministic mapping returns EA_ON for both NQ futures and NAS100 CFD. A narrow observation window is flagged around the Sunday futures restart, where thin liquidity and weekend headline risk concentrate. Existing-position management and emergency protection logic remain unaffected by this mapping.",
  "headline": "Calm Nasdaq-100 close into a weekend halt; low tail, high stress on Hormuz oil and rising global yields",
  "body": "The Nasdaq-100 cash index closed Friday 14 August at 30,046.14, down 0.13% on the session, inside a 29,934.66-30,179.82 range and roughly 2.3% below the 30,762.20 52-week high. VIX finished at 14.25 (-2.60%), consistent with subdued implied volatility rather than a stressed tape. Treasury constant-maturity yields stood at 4.15% for the 2Y and 4.63% for the 10Y at the latest H.15 publication, with commentary pointing to a broad global rise in long rates. Friday's internals were conflicting rather than directional: Broadcom fell about 5.9% on an actively exploited VMware vCenter vulnerability and cooling AI-trade sentiment, while AMD gained 6.5% and Micron 2.3%; technology as a sector was down 0.46%. That mix reduces directional conviction and is treated as a stress and confidence input, not as tail evidence.\n\nThe dominant unresolved macro channel is energy and geopolitics. The Strait of Hormuz remains effectively disrupted, Iran-Oman navigation talks are stalled, and reported attacks on Gulf tankers continued during the week. WTI settled near $82.40 (+1.42%) and Brent near $88.52, a sixth consecutive daily advance and roughly +5% on the week. This is a live discontinuity source capable of producing a weekend repricing gap, which is why stress is scored in the high band even with VIX at 14.\n\nNo live Nasdaq-100 price path exists at this timestamp: CME equity index futures are in their weekend halt and resume near 22:00 UTC. There is therefore no observed persistent one-way structure, no shallow-pullback or failed-retracement sequence, and no session-extreme behaviour to score. Under the fixed mapping this keeps tail risk low. Confidence is scored as solid rather than high because the cross-asset, calendar and catalyst evidence is complete and verified while the price-path dimension is roughly 45 hours stale.\n\nThe week ahead carries a moderate scheduled load: Empire State and NAHB on Monday, housing starts and industrial production on Tuesday, FOMC minutes on Wednesday at 18:00 UTC, claims and Philadelphia Fed on Thursday, and S&P Global flash PMIs on Friday. The Jackson Hole symposium falls on 27-29 August, outside this seven-day window.",
